07 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7770c41d-aaf3-5e25-a8be-4e4a0e8d633c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093107 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093108 #edb/ref 1061 2365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093108 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "207041d4-d02e-5136-a008-e366ceda883f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093108 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093368 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to evaluate the infinite geometric series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1} ^ \\infty 3 \\left (\\dfrac 1 4\\right) ^ n\n$$\n\nThe sum $S_\\infty$ of an infinite geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_{\\infty} = \\dfrac {\\color{black}a_1} {1 - {\\color{black}r}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}r}$ is the common ratio and $\\color{black}a_1$ is the first term. So, we need to determine $\\color{black}a_1$ and $\\color{black}r$ and then plug those values into this formula.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}a_1} = 3 \\left(\\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^1 = {\\color{black}\\dfrac 3 4}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}r} = \\dfrac {a_2}{\\color{black}a_1} = \\dfrac {3 \\left (\\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^2}{3\\left(\\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^1}= {\\color{black}\\dfrac 1 4}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3(\\frac{1}{4})^{n} &= \\frac{a_{1}}{1 - r} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3}{4}))/((1 - \\frac{1}{4})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{3}{4}))/((\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093368 #edb/ref 1065 1201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093368 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "05ae9ac9-5b25-58d0-b897-5d1d9d9507be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093368 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186232984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232983 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232983 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232983 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186232984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232983 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d78186cd-42ea-5ba4-ba88-adb813f8e3ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232984 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232984 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232984 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d7282c2a-54bb-5833-928b-ed4ac66fa331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233349 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${a}_{1}=10$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5cec570-331b-5761-a994-262c8147d70e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233349 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1-(-0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1+0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,000}{125} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233350 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233350 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233350 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${a}_{1}=-14$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233350 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf1f4594-bcb0-53bd-9558-7de1afb93bf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233350 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186232983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-350648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233987 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{\\,n-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ccdb37f-3fc9-5a29-ba10-0b973b38de6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233987 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{3-2}=6(-\\frac{1}{5})=-\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\n$$\n{a}_{4}=6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{4-2}=6(\\frac{1}{25})=\\frac{6}{25}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{25})}{(-\\frac{6}{5})}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{\\,n-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{6}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{6}{5})}{(\\frac{6}{5})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
